{0 Observe}

Observe is structured logging for OCaml. The portable [observe] package owns
point and wide-event meaning, typed and open structured authoring, policy,
bounded canonical completion, pure formatting, drains, diagnostics, and
deterministic capture without performing concrete I/O.

{1 Start here}

- {!page-getting_started} installs and initializes the ready Lwt-Unix path.
- {!page-logging} covers text, open, typed, manual, and PPX authoring.
- {!page-production} composes enrichment, bounds, redaction, sampling,
  retention, and routing without changing call sites.
- {!page-outputs} explains drains, ready filesystem delivery, flush, shutdown,
  and visible loss.
- {!page-testing} shows deterministic capture and semantic inspection.
- {!page-packages} explains the portable, runtime, and facility boundaries.

The complete installed interface is {!module:Observe}. The concise PPX forms
are optional; every logging operation also has a manual public equivalent.

{1 Core model}

A point log is one auto-emitting text, open structured, typed structured, or
explicit-error event. A wide log uses the same logging facility but accepts
successive lazy contributions before one explicit or scope-bounded completion.

Every published log starts from one finite package-owned immutable canonical
snapshot. Capture, formatters, and drains inspect that stable meaning. A drain
may apply an additional redaction policy and therefore receive an immutable
derived projection; it can never recover globally removed source data.

The portable core owns no scheduler, background worker, random generator,
wall clock, filesystem, console, or durability claim. Runtime and facility
packages supply those effects through core-defined capabilities.
